Check if an Optional expression evaluates to a fault. If so, return true if it is the expected fault, the optional if it is unexpected, or false if there was no fault and the assign happened. This can be used in like this: while (true) { char[] data; // Read until end of file if (@try_catch(data, load_line(), io::EOF)!) break; .. use data .. } In this example we read until we reach an EOF, which is expected. However, if we encounter some other fault, we rethrow is. Without this macro, the code is instead written like: while (true) { char[]? data; data = load_line(); if (catch err = data) { if (err = io::EOF) break; return err? } .. use data .. } @return True if it was the expected fault, false if the variable was assigned, otherwise returns an optional.lib/std/core/builtin.c3:497:13macrostd::core::builtin::@typeidmacro typeid @typeid(#value)
