Get the next queued event without pumping the platform. Use this to drain what is already queued without giving the platform a chance to deliver more; poll() is the usual choice. If a window is given it instead picks next queued event for that window. Events belonging to other windows are kept, not discarded; they are rotated to the back of the queue and remain available to those windows. @param window: Optionally the window to filter on @return The next eventlib/std/gfx/gfx.c3:1847:14fnstd::gfx::pollfn GfxEvent? poll(GfxWindow* window = null)
Get the next event for any window, pumping the platform first if the queue is empty. This is the normal driver for an event loop; it both pumps the platform and drains the queue. The platform is pumped at most once per drain, so looping on this until it yields nothing costs a single pump. While the user drags or resizes a window, or opens a menu, the platform runs its own loop inside this call and it does not return until that finishes. wake() cannot interrupt that. To keep drawing during it, install a set_repaint_callback callback, which is invoked from inside the platform loop. If a window is given it instead picks next queued event for that window. Events belonging to other windows are kept, not discarded; they are rotated to the back of the queue and remain available to those windows. @param window: Optionally only get events pertaining to a particular window @return The next eventlib/std/gfx/gfx.c3:1821:14fnstd::gfx::poll_monitorsfn void poll_monitors()
