Block until an event arrives, the timeout expires, or wake() is called. If an event was already queued this returns immediately without blocking. When the wait ends because a platform event arrived, that event is dispatched before returning, so it is available here rather than only on the next poll(). Returns nothing when the timeout expires, when the wait was interrupted by wake(), or when the arriving event was filtered out by the receiving window's enabled events. All three are ordinary outcomes, not errors. Blocking is not possible while the platform owns the loop -- see poll(). @param duration: How long to wait; WAIT_FOREVER to block indefinitely @return The event that ended the waitlib/std/gfx/gfx.c3:1672:14fnstd::gfx::wakefn void wake()
Interrupt a wait() in progress, which then returns with no event. Safe to call when nothing is waiting, in which case the next wait() returns immediately. This has no effect on a poll() that has entered a platform modal loop, such as an interactive resize or an open menu: that loop belongs to the window system and cannot be cancelled. Draw from a set_repaint_callback callback instead.lib/std/gfx/gfx.c3:2583:9macrostd::gfx::wgpuInstanceCreateSurfacemacro WGPUSurface wgpuInstanceCreateSurface(WGPUInstance instance, WgpuSurfaceDescriptor* descriptor)lib/std/gfx/private/gfx_wgpu.c3:16:19fnstd::gfx::write_clipboardfn void? write_clipboard(GfxDataTransfer* data)
